WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.780
Magic, mystery, and mayhem – oh my!

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.661
From sentient swords and CIA conspiracies
to warring martial arts academies, the world

00:00:08.661 --> 00:00:12.050
of the movie ninja is as vast as it is weird.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.949
For most American audiences, the term "ninja"
summons up a collection of pop culture references

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:21.600
— the long-running Teenage Mutant Ninja
Turtles franchise and Chris Farley's pratfalls

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.730
in Beverly Hills Ninja may be a couple that
come to mind.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.380
For cult movie fans, it may be the avalanche
of interchangeable 80's action movies featuring

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:34.227
a mix of Caucasian and Asian actors tossing
shuriken at bad guys.

00:00:34.227 --> 00:00:35.634
"Thank you."

00:00:37.654 --> 00:00:39.423
"That's very impressive."

00:00:40.522 --> 00:00:44.420
But the earliest movies about ninja long predated
the '80s boom.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:50.500
Japanese filmmakers first began making movies
about ninja as early as the silent film era.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.190
Many of these early titles centered around
folk tales, like those of Jiraiya and Sarutobi

00:00:55.190 --> 00:01:00.969
Sasuke — and yes, these stories directly
inspired the Naruto characters of the same name.

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:06.282
Unfortunately, like the vast majority of silent
movies, very few of these early efforts still exist.

00:01:06.282 --> 00:01:11.470
In fact, the 1921 film Jiraiya the Brave is
considered to be the oldest surviving ninja

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.500
film, though only 20 minutes of the movie
remain intact.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.840
Today, ninja are seemingly everywhere.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.020
When ninja went worldwide in the 1980s, other
countries started making their own adventures

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.590
— including Sweden, Argentina, South Korea
and India.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:30.147
There are ninja anime, giant monster movies,
comedies, and even "adults-only" films.

00:01:30.394 --> 00:01:35.829
Ninja were inescapable in Japanese movie theaters
during the 1950s and 1960s.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:40.640
The website Vintage Ninja breaks down this
period into distinct types, with one being

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.610
the fantasy-fueled efforts.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:47.490
These ninja were more like wizards, battling
evil sorcerers and giant monsters with a mix

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.909
of swordplay and spells.

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:54.960
The 1959 animated ninja film Shonen Sarutobi
Sasuke fits in this vein, as we witness the

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.770
titular proto-ninja and his fight against
a lake demon.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:02.860
Unlike many of its fellow fantasy ninja films,
it saw a theatrical release in the United

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:09.225
States — but strangely, the American version
called Magic Boy removed any references to ninja.

00:02:09.225 --> 00:02:13.950
The other ninja films of the '60s boom included
black-and-white historical action-dramas,

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:19.550
fueled by a mix of political intrigue, energetic
camerawork and bloody fight sequences.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:23.890
Some traits — such as the ninja's superhuman
stealth and their array of unique weapons

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.880
— would carry over into the brawny American
cousins of the 1980s.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:32.840
But films like the stark, psychologically
driven Samurai Spy couldn't be more different

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.183
from the action antics in the American Ninja
series.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:42.940
For many Westerners, their first exposure
to ninja came from the 1967 James Bond film,

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.390
You Only Live Twice.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:49.190
It focuses on a dissolute Bond learning ninjutsu
in Japan to carry out the assassination of

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.850
his nemesis, Blofeld.

00:02:51.182 --> 00:02:55.377
"The firing power inside my crater is enough
to annihilate a small army.

00:02:56.324 --> 00:02:58.399
You can watch it all on TV."

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:03.200
Two real-life martial artists were hired by
the film's producers to lend authenticity

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.940
to the ninja sequences.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:09.121
Judo champion Donn F. Draeger not only trained
Sean Connery in many fighting styles, but

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:11.040
also served as his stunt double.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.340
This wasn't uncommon, as Draeger had previously
served as John Wayne's stunt double in the

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.970
1958 film The Barbarian and the Geisha.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:23.500
The other consultant was Masaaki Hatsumi — recognized
today as a grandmaster of the nine schools

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.800
of martial arts — who also appeared in the
movie as an aide to the spy Tiger Tanaka.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:32.310
Hatsumi later used his training to found a
dojo in Japan, where thousands travel each

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.786
year to learn ninjutsu techniques.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.450
By the mid-1970s, ninja had infiltrated American
popular culture.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:44.950
They appeared on television series, like Kung
Fu and Hawaii Five-0, while Marvel and DC

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:50.239
introduced ninja characters in Judomaster,
Shang-Chi: Master of Kung Fu and the Deadly

00:03:50.239 --> 00:03:51.830
Hands of Kung Fu anthology.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:57.540
However, ninja wouldn't turn up in a Hollywood
movie until 1975's The Killer Elite.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.159
Directed by action auteur Sam Peckinpah, the
movie concerns a pair of undercover agents

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:05.159
for hire, played by James Caan and Robert
Duvall.

00:04:05.159 --> 00:04:10.020
Their testosterone-fueled friendship comes
to an abrupt end when Duvall double-crosses

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.269
Caan and leaves him for dead.

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:16.810
Caan's rehabilitation involves intense martial
arts training, which serves him well when

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:21.340
he hits the revenge trail to stop an assassin
from killing a Taiwanese politician.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:26.100
This doesn't sit well with Duvall, who summons
a small army of ninja to finish the job.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.919
But Peckinpah's ninja are no more effective
than standard issue henchmen, falling hard

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:36.170
to a guy with a cane, a dumpy Burt Young,
a middle-aged diplomat and his daughter, and

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.572
a machine gun-toting Bo Hopkins.

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:43.370
According to the martial arts compendium These
Fists Break Bricks, the early 1980s ninja

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:48.440
were thriving on TV, in best-selling books,
and even in perfume.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:53.270
The time was right for a Hollywood ninja movie,
and Mike Stone thought it would be his ticket

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:54.270
to stardom.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:59.300
Stone, a karate champion and instructor, pitched
a ninja movie called Dance of Death to The

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:03.280
Cannon Group — the movie factory that would
be responsible for many of Chuck Norris's

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.400
action titles.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.330
With Stone signed on as both star and scriptwriter,
the producers were hoping to beat 20th Century

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:13.340
Fox's adaptation of Eric Van Lustbader's The
Ninja to theaters.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:17.500
They didn't need to worry — that production
fell into developmental hell — but they

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:23.120
soon faced their own problems with the movie
that would become 1981's Enter the Ninja.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.960
Producer Menahem Golan reportedly arrived
on set and fired the director, crew, and Stone

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.210
as leading man.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:32.160
As Stone said in The Cannon Film Guide Volume
II,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:42.449
Golan stepped in as director and hired Italian
actor Franco Nero to replace Stone.

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:42.949
"Gentlemen.

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:44.489
Let's be reasonable.

00:05:44.489 --> 00:05:45.404
Why don't we talk?"

00:05:47.449 --> 00:05:52.520
Golan would eventually walk back his decision
somewhat, rehiring Stone as fight coordinator

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:54.110
and as Nero's stunt double.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:58.520
He allowed Stone to hire his own stuntmen,
including a Japanese kendo and judo champ

00:05:58.520 --> 00:05:59.990
named Sho Kosugi.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:04.540
Kosugi would also play one of the film's bad
guys — a performance that made him the face

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:06.833
of ninja throughout the 1980s.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:13.940
Throughout the 80s, Sho Kosugi was an on-screen
murder machine, laying waste to every bad

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:16.070
guy who dared to cross his path.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:21.030
One of his most popular movies during this
period was 1985's Pray for Death.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:25.349
That was the best you could hope for from
Sho: that he'd kill you quickly.

00:06:25.349 --> 00:06:30.100
That image contrasted heavily with Kosugi's
real life as a hard-working family man with

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:33.330
a healthy skepticism about his status as an
action hero.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:38.160
Though his on-screen characters made him exceptionally
popular, his legacy was ultimately cemented

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.970
as the first Japanese actor to top-bill an
American action movie.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:47.751
While he had been elevated from the bad guy
in Enter the Ninja to the hero in Revenge

00:06:47.751 --> 00:06:52.800
of the Ninja, he was dismissive of the loopy
Ninja III: The Domination and left the Cannon

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.690
Group over a contract dispute.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:59.150
After Pray for Death earned an X rating, he
requested cuts to ensure that younger fans

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:00.150
could see it.

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:04.661
When the national box office returns for ninja
movies cooled off, Kosugi hosted the Ninja

00:07:04.661 --> 00:07:09.979
Theater VHS series and eventually returned
to Japan, where he found new fame on television.

00:07:09.979 --> 00:07:15.340
In 2009, the Wachowskis paid tribute to his
early screen days by casting him as the villain

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:16.580
in Ninja Assassin.

00:07:16.992 --> 00:07:21.240
Though The Cannon Group lost Sho Kosugi, they
still saw money to be made in the shuriken

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.530
and katana business.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:28.414
As noted in These Fist Break Bricks, the company
pre-sold American Ninja as a vehicle for Chuck Norris.

00:07:28.414 --> 00:07:32.912
They had to go looking for a new action star,
though, as Norris wasn't interested in the project.

00:07:32.912 --> 00:07:37.930
Enter Michael Dudikoff, a model-turned-actor
who often played affable boyfriends and best

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.139
pals in movies and on television.

00:07:40.548 --> 00:07:41.548
"Veal…

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:42.880
Par…

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:44.639
What's this word?"

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:46.108
"It's Parmesan."

00:07:46.108 --> 00:07:51.414
Director Sam Firstenberg was instantly smitten
with Dudikoff, recalling to KungFuMagazine.com,

00:07:59.367 --> 00:08:02.379
What Dudikoff lacked was the necessary martial
arts skills.

00:08:02.379 --> 00:08:07.050
He knew some, but he wasn't entirely believable
as someone who could fight multiple ninja

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.509
at once or jump out of a helicopter.

00:08:09.509 --> 00:08:13.500
Thankfully, the producers still had Mike Stone
on hand to choreograph the fights and help

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.860
Dudikoff achieve ninja-level prowess.

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.780
It certainly didn't hurt that he was paired
up on-screen with Steve James, a brawny real-life

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.009
martial artist.

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:26.419
Dudikoff would play Joe Armstrong in two of
the three sequels, sharing lead billing with

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:29.000
actor David Bradley on American Ninja 4.

00:08:29.463 --> 00:08:34.380
Let's say it's 1982, and you're an independent
distributor with a sizable catalog of martial

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.197
arts movies.

00:08:35.197 --> 00:08:39.060
Audiences aren't lining up for these titles
like they used to — not like the new crop

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:41.810
of ninja flicks cleaning up in theaters and
on home video.

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:46.060
So, being the enterprising type, you snip
off the title cards from a handful of your

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:49.709
movies and stitch on new ones that prominently
feature ninja.

00:08:49.709 --> 00:08:53.893
The fact that your movies have little or nothing
to do with ninja, well, that's beside the point.

00:08:53.893 --> 00:08:59.400
According to a 1986 New York Daily News interview
with Rod Hurley, Vice President of Master Arts Video,

00:09:03.978 --> 00:09:07.900
One of the more amusing examples includes
1982's Shaolin Prince.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:13.279
When it finally arrived on American VHS, it
bore the title Death Mask of the Ninja, despite

00:09:13.279 --> 00:09:15.410
no ninja being in the film.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:20.769
The 1978 period action drama Heroes of the
East features Chinese and Japanese martial

00:09:20.769 --> 00:09:23.660
arts, but its real concern is a broken marriage.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:29.660
Nevertheless, it was renamed numerous times,
given titles such as Shaolin Challenges Ninja,

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.592
Challenge of the Ninja, and Shaolin vs. Ninja.

00:09:32.902 --> 00:09:37.914
When a film subgenre runs out of gas, one
of the most common ways for producers to retain

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:41.950
fan interest is to fold elements of other
genres into their titles.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:48.060
This cross-pollination has resulted in some
inspired hybrids and some genuine oddities,

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:50.150
and ninja movies are no exception.

00:09:50.303 --> 00:09:51.322
"Curses!

00:09:51.322 --> 00:09:53.084
Get 'em, boys!"

00:09:55.819 --> 00:10:02.370
During this period, fans would stumble across
mutations like 1989's Robot Ninja, a no-budget

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:05.190
comedy about a comic book artist fighting
crime.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:09.220
Ninja Academy, from the same year, applied
the Police Academy formula to a pair of rival

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:12.160
ninja schools populated by misanthropic weirdos.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:20.220
Of course, the hands-down winner for most
bizarre ninja-hybrid movie might just be 1984's

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.190
Ninja III: The Domination.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:27.956
Intended as another sequel to Enter the Ninja,
the film takes an unexpected turn into the supernatural.

00:10:27.956 --> 00:10:32.180
Lucinda Dickey is an aerobics instructor possessed
by the spirit of an evil ninja.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:36.930
James Hong is an exorcist who fails to remove
the spirit, which prompts — who else?

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:38.240
— Sho Kosugi to step in.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:45.602
Floating swords, lycra bodysuits, and mind
control all combine into a truly absurd experience.

00:10:45.984 --> 00:10:50.690
While it's unlikely that the American-made
ninja movies of the 1980s will ever be described

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:56.260
as classics, even the worst Cannon Group titles
can appear worthy of the Criterion Collection

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:59.440
when compared to the films of Hong Kong's
Godfrey Ho.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:04.580
He flooded international markets with hundreds
of cheaply made, nonsensical ninja movies

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:06.889
during the 1980s and 1990s.

00:11:07.094 --> 00:11:10.710
"Alright, you better come out now."

00:11:11.988 --> 00:11:15.771
"Oh, I think we better do as he says."

00:11:15.771 --> 00:11:20.690
After catching wind of Enter the Ninja and
wanting his own Franco Nero for his ninja

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:25.399
projects, Ho would hire American actor Richard
Harrison — a star of spaghetti Westerns.

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:29.490
However, Ho didn't seem to care that Harrison
didn't know martial arts.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.380
He also didn't care that the props and sets
were dollar-store quality, or that the movies'

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:37.480
dialogue was seemingly endless verbal loops
on ninja empires.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:41.980
Ho simply hid his stunt players behind hoods
and masks, and threw in plenty of gunplay

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:43.980
and smoke bombs to hide the seams.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:48.290
But what set Ho's movies apart from other
low-budget martial arts titles of the era

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:53.829
was his practice of recycling footage from
one film to create dozens of others.

00:11:53.829 --> 00:11:58.620
Scenes of Harrison shot for one movie would
turn up in several other Ho titles, and Harrison

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:02.730
later claimed that Ho's movies caused him
to quit acting completely.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:06.970
When Ho exhausted his footage of Harrison,
he bought movies from other Asian markets

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:10.440
and dissected them for more Frankenstein creations.

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:15.610
Quality — or lack thereof — didn't stop
Ho until he supposedly retired in the early

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:19.212
2000s to become a film teacher, of all things.

00:12:19.493 --> 00:12:25.649
In the 1980s, ninja exploitation films wreaked
low-budget havoc across international screens

00:12:25.649 --> 00:12:28.852
in the Philippines, Hong Kong, Turkey, and
even India.

00:12:28.852 --> 00:12:32.860
But one probably wouldn't expect a ninja movie
to come from Sweden.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:36.940
However, the Scandinavian country produced
a small handful of homegrown ninja titles

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:42.820
in the late 80s, with the best known to international
audiences being 1984's The Ninja Mission.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:46.621
"Listen Mason, and listen good.

00:12:46.621 --> 00:12:51.910
You're to take the ninja into Russia, and
get Markov out."

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:58.070
A hopelessly convoluted and astonishingly
gory thriller that pits a CIA-backed team

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:04.250
of Swedish ninja against Soviet forces that
hold a defecting Russian scientist hostage.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:09.740
Bo F. Munthe — the self-described Grandfather
of European Ninjutsu — co-stars as a member

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:14.680
of the ninja team, whose martial arts skills
could be charitably described as rudimentary.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:19.449
However, their ability to kill bad guys in
as messy a manner as possible is top-notch.

00:13:19.449 --> 00:13:23.640
Director Mats Helge Olsson had previously
made headlines in the early 80s for producing

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:29.778
the historical epic Sweden for the Swedes,
which landed him in prison for accounting misdeeds.

00:13:29.778 --> 00:13:34.899
He rebounded with the international theatrical
and VHS sales for The Ninja Mission, which

00:13:34.899 --> 00:13:40.920
he followed with a slew of other action titles
— each one as technically inept, yet enthusiastically

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.662
made as the next.

00:13:42.662 --> 00:13:48.160
The VHS boom wasn't just a fresh revenue source
for theatrical movies, old and new.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:52.360
It also provided aspiring filmmakers a chance
to get their homemade productions out into

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:53.360
the world.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:57.769
These backyard auteurs typically focused on
the genres that attracted the widest viewership

00:13:57.769 --> 00:14:02.149
— so if you owned a headband and a rubber
throwing star, this was your chance to be

00:14:02.149 --> 00:14:03.760
in the ninja movie business.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:08.689
Some of the best-known, or notorious, homegrown
ninja titles included Justice, Ninja Style

00:14:08.689 --> 00:14:14.930
— a $12,000 actioner by St. Louis detective-turned-filmmaker
Ron White — and L.A. Streetfighters, where

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:19.830
a forty-something former Bruce Lee clone plays
a high schooler battling drug dealers.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:24.259
But L.A. Streetfighters director Richard Park
Woo-sang also oversaw another amateur ninja

00:14:24.259 --> 00:14:29.570
picture, Miami Connection, that inexplicably
found an enthusiastic audience — three decades

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:31.370
after its original release.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.589
According to Park, after seeing an interview
with taekwondo studio owner Master Y.K.

00:14:35.589 --> 00:14:39.829
Kim on a South Korean talk show, Park pitched
him on making a martial arts movie.

00:14:39.829 --> 00:14:44.320
Kim recruited his students as cast members,
borrowed money from friends, and even mortgaged

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.210
his school to fund the movie.

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:50.500
However, distributors took one look at its
plot — a mishmash of non-sequiturs about

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:55.750
a rock band running afoul of drug-dealing
ninja — and advised Kim to just throw it away.

00:14:55.750 --> 00:15:00.550
Flash forward to 2009, when Alamo Drafthouse
programmer Zach Carlson bought the film on

00:15:00.550 --> 00:15:04.570
eBay and screened a reel for audiences at
their Austin, Texas location.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:10.430
Rapturous response led to festival dates,
leaving Kim baffled, delighted, and ready

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:11.740
to make more movies.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:12.740
He told Weekly,

00:15:19.388 --> 00:15:24.410
Taiwanese actor John Liu earned a following
for his superhuman high kicks in low-budget

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:28.410
Hong Kong and Taiwanese kung fu movies during
the 1970s.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:32.110
But as the old joke goes, what he really wanted
to do was direct.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:37.250
In the 1980s, he established his own microbudget
production company and wrote, produced, directed,

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:41.130
and starred in four nearly incoherent martial
arts movies.

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:46.180
The last of these, New York Ninja, began filming
in 1984 but was abandoned after its distributor

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:47.730
filed for bankruptcy.

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:53.248
This drove Liu to give up filmmaking and return
to Paris to oversee his own martial arts studio.

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:57.790
"You can't beat me.

00:15:57.790 --> 00:15:59.483
I'm immortal."

00:15:59.483 --> 00:16:03.949
Flash-forward three decades, and the film
preservation and home video distributor Vinegar

00:16:03.949 --> 00:16:08.630
Syndrome locates the surviving elements, but
no script or storyboards.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:13.279
When Liu declined to complete the movie, editor
Kurtis M. Spieler painstakingly assembled

00:16:13.279 --> 00:16:19.699
the footage into a semi-coherent storyline,
earning himself credit as the film's Re-Director.

00:16:19.699 --> 00:16:24.400
After corralling several cult actors to dub
the on-screen characters, New York Ninja played

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:29.529
the film festival circuit in 2021, eventually
receiving a national release.

00:16:29.529 --> 00:16:30.850
As for the finished product?

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:33.680
Well, seeing really is believing.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:37.970
Audiences may not fully accept the sight of
a ninja vigilante patrolling New York City

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:42.019
— occasionally on roller skates — in search
of the cartoonish gang members who killed

00:16:42.019 --> 00:16:43.019
his wife.

00:16:43.019 --> 00:16:47.449
A radioactive villain called the Plutonium
Man and an army of grade school ninja fans

00:16:47.449 --> 00:16:53.681
also factor into the mix, which wobbles and
bops to a new '80s-synth-style score by Voyag3r.

00:16:54.108 --> 00:16:59.069
Though ninja appeared to be unstoppable on-screen,
their appeal eventually waned.

00:16:59.069 --> 00:17:03.230
While remaining a popular draw on the home
video market, theatrical returns began to

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.581
dwindle as the 80s drew to a close.

00:17:05.581 --> 00:17:10.040
A wave of negative press started, about how
ninja led to real-life crime, including a

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:15.010
highly-publicized 1984 home invasion of actress/director
Penny Marshall.

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:21.130
Profit-minded producers began shifting away
from ninja, wi th films like 1985's No Retreat,

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:25.829
No Surrender elevating the marquee status
of kickboxing, a more aggressive combat sport

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:28.690
than ninjutsu, with less spirituality.

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:33.809
With the help of the energetic Belgian martial
artist and actor Jean-Claude Van Damme, kickboxing

00:17:33.809 --> 00:17:38.570
and mixed martial arts came to dominate the
American action scene, introducing a host

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:40.010
of new movie stars.

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:44.410
These ranged from Steven Seagal and Bruce
Lee's son, Brandon, to direct-to-video players

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:48.039
like Billy Blanks, Don "The Dragon" Wilson,
and Cynthia Rothrock.

